---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL, CLAIMS } from '../lib/site.js';
import { LICENCE_STATES } from '../lib/licensing.js';
import { getSiteStats, formatCount } from '../lib/data.js';
import attribution from '../data/overture-attribution.json';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Editorial guidelines', url: '/editorial-guidelines/' },
];
const stats = getSiteStats();
const states = Object.values(LICENCE_STATES);
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How We Choose and Verify Listings"
  description="How GarageDoorHQ builds its directory: the data we use, the bar a listing must clear, what a licence badge means, and why we publish no ratings."
  canonical="/editorial-guidelines/"
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <article style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <h1 style="margin:1rem 0 .5rem;">Editorial guidelines</h1>
      <p class="muted" style="margin:0 0 2rem;font-size:.85rem;">
        Last updated: {LEGAL.lastUpdated}
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This page exists so that nothing about how the directory works is a mystery. If
          something here does not match what you see on a page, that is a bug and we want
          to know: <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.contactEmail}`}>{LEGAL.contactEmail}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Nobody pays to be here</h2>
        <p>
          We do not sell listings, placement, ranking, badges or removal from a competitor's
          city page. There is no paid tier and no "featured" slot for sale. The site is
          funded by advertising and, in future, affiliate arrangements — neither of which
          affects which businesses are listed or in what order.
        </p>
        <p>
          Listing is free, and <a href="/get-listed/">anyone can ask to be added</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the listing data comes from</h2>
        <p>
          Business names, addresses, phone numbers, websites, coordinates and categories
          come from <strong>{attribution.source}</strong>, release {attribution.release},
          published under the {attribution.license}.
        </p>
        <p>
          That choice is deliberate and it is a legal one. An Apache 2.0 licensed dataset
          may lawfully be stored, republished and redistributed. Google Places data may not
          — its terms forbid rehosting, and buying the same data from a scraper does not
          change that, because the restriction follows the data rather than the method of
          acquisition. We do not use it, in any form, from any intermediary.
        </p>

        <h2>Why there are no star ratings on this site</h2>
        <p>
          Because there is no rating source we are allowed to republish. The dataset above
          contains no ratings and no reviews. The datasets that do contain them forbid
          storing and reproducing them.
        </p>
        <p>
          So a five-star average on this site could only have come from somewhere we are not
          entitled to take it from, or from us inventing it. Rather than do either, we
          publish no ratings at all and do not rank contractors by quality. The trade-off is
          real and we would rather state it than paper over it.
        </p>
        <p>
          For the same reason there are no opening hours. Where we ever show an availability
          claim, the wording will be "advertises 24/7" rather than "open 24/7", because
          {' '}{CLAIMS.hoursMeaning.replace(/^Where a listing says a contractor advertises 24\/7 service, that claim/, 'the claim')}
        </p>

        <h2>The quality bar a listing must clear</h2>
        <p>A record is only published if all of the following are true:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Identified by category, not by name.</strong> The business is categorised as a garage door service in the source data. A business matched only because its name contains "garage door" is quarantined for human review, never auto-published — a name search alone returns storage companies, car washes, a gas station and, in one case, a condominium.</li>
          <li><strong>Currently operating.</strong> The source marks the business as open. Closed records are dropped.</li>
          <li><strong>Contactable.</strong> A phone number is present. A listing nobody can ring does not do the one job it exists to do.</li>
          <li><strong>Locatable.</strong> A city is present, taken from the record's own address rather than inferred from a map bounding box.</li>
          <li><strong>In scope.</strong> Storage units, parking operators, car washes, auto body shops and commercial-only rolling-steel and loading-dock suppliers are excluded. This site is about residential garage doors.</li>
          <li><strong>In a city with enough listings.</strong> A city page needs at least three listings to publish. One or two makes a page too thin to be useful. Held-back records are not deleted — they publish automatically when the city reaches the threshold.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          {formatCount(stats.businesses)} listings currently clear that bar, across
          {' '}{formatCount(stats.cities)} cities. Every one has a phone number, because
          that is a condition of publishing.
        </p>

        <h2>What a licence badge means</h2>
        <p>
          Garage door work is licensed as a distinct trade in exactly three states:
        </p>
        <ul>
          {states.map((s) => (
            <li><strong>{s.state}</strong> — {s.authority}, {s.classification}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>
          In those three states a listing may show a <strong>✓ State licence verified</strong>
          {' '}badge. That badge means precisely this: we matched a licence number to a
          record on that state's public register on the date shown on the listing.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>{CLAIMS.licenceMeaning}</strong> We do not verify insurance, pricing,
          qualifications or the quality of anyone's work. We do not visit businesses. We do
          not re-check a licence between data refreshes, which is why the check date is
          published and why we tell you to check the number yourself — the register is free
          and takes about a minute.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the other 47 states there is no garage-door-specific register, so no listing
          carries a badge. The absence of a badge outside California, Florida and Arizona
          means nothing about the business. Those pages explain what to ask for instead,
          which is proof of liability insurance.
        </p>

        <h2>How the cost guides are written</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Figures are national 2026 estimates researched in-house. They are ranges, not quotes, and every guide says so.</li>
          <li>Every guide carries a visible last-updated date.</li>
          <li><strong>Figures must agree across the site.</strong> A cost quoted on a city page or a business page is read from the guide that owns it, not retyped — so the two cannot drift apart. When a guide's figure changes, everywhere that quotes it changes with it.</li>
          <li>We do not publish two pages targeting the same question. Two of our own pages competing for one search means the weaker one wins, which serves nobody.</li>
          <li>We publish a guide when it is finished, not as a stub. There are no placeholder pages on this site.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Safety comes before completeness</h2>
        <p>
          We do not publish instructions for garage door spring or cable work, and we will
          not, regardless of search demand for them. A wound torsion spring holds enough
          stored energy to cause serious injury, and a partial instruction is more dangerous
          than none. Where the honest answer to "how do I do this" is "you should not", the
          page says that and stops.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          If a figure, a listing or a licence detail is wrong, email
          {' '}<a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.contactEmail}`}>{LEGAL.contactEmail}</a>. We correct
          errors on the page rather than quietly deleting them, and if a listing is removed
          its URL redirects to the city page rather than returning an error.
        </p>
        <p>
          {SITE.name} is run by {LEGAL.founderName}, {LEGAL.founderRole} — see
          {' '}<a href="/about/">about</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
